🧩 Main Classes
1. JiraFlowCoordinator

JiraFlowCoordinator is the main orchestration class of the integration. It is responsible for centralizing the base configuration, resolving the correct report folder, identifying the suite name, generating the execution and Test Plan names, finding the XML result file, collecting failure image candidates, and triggering the full Jira publishing flow in the correct order by calling the report builder, Test Plan manager, and execution publisher. This is the right file to update when you need to change global Jira settings, naming rules, XML priority, report folder resolution, or the overall execution sequence. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

2. JiraGateway

JiraGateway is the Jira communication layer and contains the logic that actually sends requests to Jira and interprets the responses. It validates the required configuration, imports XML execution results, searches for existing Test Plans, retrieves issues by key, creates new Test Plans, links Test Executions to Test Plans, updates execution metadata, and uploads attachments. This is the file to change when you need to adjust request behavior, authentication, response parsing, accepted status codes, or add new Jira API actions. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

3. JiraTestPlanManager

JiraTestPlanManager is responsible for deciding whether the current execution should reuse an existing Test Plan or create a new one. It checks force-create rules, manual overrides, previously saved Test Plan keys, validates whether a saved key still matches the current suite, searches Jira for an exact matching Test Plan, selects the newest valid result, and saves the resolved key locally for future reuse. This is the correct file to update when you want to change the reuse strategy, matching rules, saved key validation, or the conditions for creating a new Test Plan. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

4. JiraExecutionPublisher

JiraExecutionPublisher handles the publishing stage after the Test Plan has already been resolved. It imports execution results into Jira, adds the created execution to the Test Plan, updates the execution summary, labels, and environment, locates the generated GAIA report, creates Results.zip and Evidences.zip, uploads all attachments, and skips locked, unreadable, or excluded files during packaging. This is the file to update when you need to change ZIP content rules, attachment upload order, excluded folders or files, or the publishing sequence after execution creation. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

5. JiraGaiaReportBuilder

JiraGaiaReportBuilder is responsible for generating the custom gaiaReport.html file that is later attached to the Jira Test Execution. It parses XML execution results and execution logs, builds and enriches scenario data, creates fallback scenarios when log information is missing, normalizes scenario steps, applies timing fallback logic, finds and attaches failed evidence images, and prepares the final model used by the HTML template before writing the report file into the report folder. This is the file to change when you need to adjust log parsing, scenario extraction, fallback behavior, timing calculation, screenshot matching, error summary logic, or the report generation flow before rendering. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]





🌐 Jira Endpoints
1. Import Results

This endpoint is used to import the XML execution results into Jira and automatically create the Test Execution issue based on the test result file generated by Katalon. It is called by JiraGateway.importExecutionResults() through the Katalon object Jira Integration/Import Results, using the endpoint POST /rest/raven/1.0/import/execution/junit?projectKey=${project}. This is the endpoint to review when execution import fails, the XML format changes, or the response no longer returns the expected execution key. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

2. Create Test Plan

This endpoint is used to create a new Jira issue of type Test Plan when no valid existing Test Plan is found for the current suite. It is called by JiraGateway.createTestPlan() through the Katalon object Jira Integration/Create Test Plan, using the endpoint POST /rest/api/2/issue, and it sends the project key, summary, issue type, and Epic relation. This is the endpoint to update when the Jira field mapping changes, the Epic field changes, or the Test Plan creation payload must include more fields. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

3. Search Test Plans

This endpoint is used to search Jira for existing Test Plans so the integration can reuse the correct one instead of creating duplicates. It is called by JiraGateway.searchTestPlans() using a runtime RequestObject with the endpoint GET /rest/api/2/search, and the returned issues are later filtered by exact summary inside JiraTestPlanManager. This is the endpoint logic to review when the search is returning too many results, missing the expected Test Plan, or when the JQL needs to become more specific. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

4. Get Issue By Key

This endpoint is used to validate whether a previously saved Test Plan key still points to the correct Jira issue before reusing it in a new execution. It is called by JiraGateway.getIssueByKey() using the endpoint GET /rest/api/2/issue/{issueKey}?fields=summary,created,issuetype, and it is mainly used by JiraTestPlanManager during saved key validation. This is the endpoint to review when saved Test Plan reuse becomes unreliable or when additional validation fields are needed. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

5. Update Test Plan

This endpoint is used to link the newly created Test Execution to the resolved Jira Test Plan after the XML import is completed. It is called by JiraGateway.addExecutionToTestPlan() through the Katalon object Jira Integration/Update Test Plan, using the endpoint POST /rest/raven/2.0/api/testplan/${testPlanKey}/testexecution, and it sends the execution key in the request body. This is the endpoint to update when the Xray linking contract changes or when executions need to be added to the Test Plan in a different way. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

6. Update Test Execution Name

This endpoint is used to update the Jira Test Execution after it is created, mainly to set the execution summary, environment, and labels according to the current run. It is called by JiraGateway.updateTestExecution() through the Katalon object Jira Integration/Update Test Execution Name, using the endpoint PUT /rest/api/2/issue/${issueID}. This is the endpoint to review when the execution metadata needs to change, more Jira fields must be updated, or labels and environment handling are modified. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

7. Evidence attachment

This endpoint is used to upload attachments to the Jira Test Execution, including the generated gaiaReport.html, Evidences.zip, and Results.zip files. It is called by JiraGateway.uploadAttachment() through the Katalon object Jira Integration/Evidence attachment, using the endpoint POST /rest/api/2/issue/${execID}/attachments. This is the endpoint to review when attachment upload fails, file validation rules change, or new artifact types need to be uploaded. 🔗 Full page: [Add link here]

